// Lower secondary science (Year 1 and Year 2 / Grade 7 and 8), following the four lower secondary themes:
// Diversity, Models, Systems and Interactions. Same formats as questions.js (T) and lessons.js (L).

/* ===================== YEAR 1 ===================== */
T("prac","l1-endeavour","The scientific endeavour",1,[
["What is the first step of a scientific investigation?","Ask a question you can test","Write the conclusion","Draw the graph","Repeat the experiment","Investigations start with a testable question, then a hypothesis, a fair test, results and a conclusion."],
["What is a hypothesis?","A testable prediction with a reason","A proven fact","A list of apparatus","The final result","A hypothesis is a prediction you can test, e.g. 'If the water is warmer, the sugar dissolves faster because particles move faster.'"],
["Which hazard symbol means a substance can catch fire easily?","Flammable (flame)","Corrosive","Toxic (skull)","Irritant","The flame symbol means flammable. Keep such substances away from Bunsen flames."],
["Why do we repeat an experiment and take an average?","To make the results more reliable","To make the experiment longer","To change the variable","To get a different answer","Repeating reduces the effect of random errors, so the average is more reliable."],
["You spill acid on your hand in the lab. What should you do first?","Rinse it with plenty of running water and tell the teacher","Wipe it on your clothes","Add alkali to your hand","Ignore it if it doesn't hurt","Rinse straight away with lots of water, then report it so the teacher can help."],
["What should you do when using a Bunsen burner and it is not being used for heating?","Turn it to the yellow safety flame","Turn the gas off at the burner only","Leave it on the blue flame","Cover it with paper","The yellow flame is easy to see, so no one burns themselves on an invisible blue flame."]
]);
T("chem","l1-physical","Physical properties of matter",1,[
["A block has mass 40 g and volume 20 cm³. What is its density?","2 g/cm³","0.5 g/cm³","800 g/cm³","60 g/cm³","Density = mass ÷ volume = 40 ÷ 20 = 2 g/cm³."],
["An object will float in water if its density is…","less than 1 g/cm³","more than 1 g/cm³","exactly 10 g/cm³","the same as its mass","Water's density is 1 g/cm³. Less dense objects float."],
["Which property makes copper suitable for electrical wires?","It is a good conductor of electricity","It is brittle","It has a low melting point","It is magnetic","Copper conducts electricity well and is ductile, so it can be drawn into wires."],
["Why are cooking pots often made of metal with plastic handles?","Metal conducts heat, plastic is a poor conductor","Plastic is stronger than metal","Metal is lighter than plastic","Plastic conducts heat well","The metal pot passes heat to the food; the plastic handle insulates your hand."],
["What does 'malleable' mean?","Can be hammered into shapes without breaking","Can be stretched into wires","Breaks easily when hit","Lets light pass through","Malleable materials, like most metals, can be hammered or pressed into shape."],
["How can you find the volume of an irregular stone?","Lower it into a measuring cylinder of water and read the rise","Measure its length only","Weigh it on a balance","Heat it and measure the temperature","The water rises by a volume equal to the stone's volume (displacement method)."]
]);
T("chem","l1-elements","Elements, compounds and mixtures",1,[
["What is an element?","A substance made of only one type of atom","A substance made of two elements joined","Any mixture of substances","A liquid that conducts electricity","Elements cannot be broken down into simpler substances by chemical means."],
["Which of these is a compound?","Water (H₂O)","Oxygen (O₂)","Air","Iron (Fe)","Water is hydrogen and oxygen chemically combined. Air is a mixture; O₂ and Fe are elements."],
["Which is a mixture?","Seawater","Carbon dioxide","Sodium chloride","Copper","Seawater is salt and other substances in water, not chemically joined, so it can be separated physically."],
["Iron and sulfur are heated together and form iron sulfide. Which statement is true?","Iron sulfide has different properties from iron and sulfur","Iron sulfide is still magnetic like iron","Iron sulfide is a mixture","Iron sulfide can be separated with a magnet","A compound has new properties. Iron sulfide is not magnetic and needs a chemical reaction to separate."],
["How are the elements arranged in the Periodic Table?","By increasing proton (atomic) number","By colour","Alphabetically","By date of discovery","Elements are in order of proton number; metals are on the left, non-metals on the right."],
["Which is a property of most non-metals?","Poor conductor of heat and electricity","Shiny and malleable","High density","Good conductor of electricity","Most non-metals are dull, brittle when solid and poor conductors (graphite is an exception)."]
]);
T("chem","l1-separation","Separation techniques",1,[
["Which method separates sand from water?","Filtration","Distillation","Chromatography","Evaporation only","Sand is insoluble, so it stays on the filter paper as the residue while water passes through as the filtrate."],
["How do you get salt crystals from salt solution?","Evaporation or crystallisation","Filtration","Using a magnet","Chromatography","Heating removes the water, leaving the salt behind."],
["Which method gives pure water from seawater?","Simple distillation","Filtration","Chromatography","Magnetic attraction","Water boils, then condenses in the condenser, leaving dissolved salt behind."],
["Which method separates the coloured dyes in ink?","Paper chromatography","Filtration","Distillation","Sieving","Different dyes travel different distances up the paper with the solvent."],
["Which method separates ethanol from water?","Fractional distillation","Filtration","Using a magnet","Decanting","Ethanol (78 °C) and water (100 °C) have different boiling points, so they can be separated by fractional distillation."],
["In chromatography, why is the start line drawn in pencil?","Pencil does not dissolve in the solvent","Pencil is darker","Ink is too expensive","Pencil moves with the solvent","Ink would dissolve and spread with the solvent, mixing with the results."]
]);
T("bio","l1-diversity","Diversity and classification of living things",1,[
["Why do scientists classify living things?","To group organisms by shared characteristics so they are easier to study","To make them all the same","To count how many there are","To decide which are the most useful","Classification organises the huge diversity of life into groups with common features."],
["What does a dichotomous key do?","Identifies organisms using pairs of yes/no choices","Measures an organism's mass","Shows a food chain","Lists the cell parts","Each step splits into two choices until you reach the organism's name."],
["Which characteristic do all mammals share?","They have fur or hair and feed young with milk","They lay eggs in water","They have scales","They are cold-blooded","Mammals have hair or fur and mammary glands."],
["A spider has eight legs and two body parts. Which group is it in?","Arachnids","Insects","Crustaceans","Mammals","Insects have six legs and three body parts; arachnids have eight legs."],
["Which group of plants makes seeds inside fruits?","Flowering plants","Ferns","Mosses","Algae","Flowering plants enclose seeds in fruits. Ferns and mosses reproduce by spores."],
["Fungi are not plants because…","they cannot photosynthesise","they are always green","they have leaves and roots","they make flowers","Fungi have no chlorophyll. They feed on dead or living material."]
]);
T("bio","l1-cells","Cells: the units of life",1,[
["Which structure controls the activities of the cell?","Nucleus","Cell wall","Vacuole","Cell membrane","The nucleus contains genetic material (DNA) and controls the cell."],
["Which structures are found in plant cells but NOT animal cells?","Cell wall and chloroplasts","Nucleus and cytoplasm","Cell membrane and nucleus","Cytoplasm and mitochondria","Plant cells have a cellulose cell wall, chloroplasts and a large central vacuole."],
["What does the cell membrane do?","Controls what enters and leaves the cell","Makes food by photosynthesis","Gives the cell a fixed shape","Stores genetic information","The partially permeable membrane controls the movement of substances."],
["Why does a root hair cell have a long thin extension?","To increase the surface area for absorbing water","To photosynthesise","To carry oxygen","To move the plant","A larger surface area means faster absorption of water and mineral salts."],
["Put the levels of organisation in order, smallest first.","Cell → tissue → organ → organ system → organism","Organ → cell → tissue → organism","Tissue → cell → organ → system","Organism → organ → tissue → cell","Similar cells form tissues, tissues form organs, organs form systems."],
["Red blood cells have no nucleus. How does this help them?","More space to carry oxygen","They can divide faster","They can make their own food","They become stronger","Without a nucleus there is more room for haemoglobin, which carries oxygen."]
]);
T("chem","l1-particles","Particulate model of matter",1,[
["How are particles arranged in a solid?","Closely packed in a regular pattern, vibrating in fixed positions","Far apart and moving randomly","Close together but sliding past each other","Not moving at all, with gaps","Solid particles are tightly packed and only vibrate, so solids have a fixed shape."],
["Why can gases be compressed easily?","There are large spaces between the particles","The particles are very small","The particles are stuck together","Gas particles do not move","The particles are far apart, so they can be pushed closer together."],
["What is the change from liquid to gas at the boiling point called?","Boiling","Melting","Condensation","Freezing","Boiling happens throughout the liquid at the boiling point. Evaporation happens at the surface at any temperature."],
["You can smell perfume from across the room. Which process explains this?","Diffusion","Condensation","Melting","Freezing","Perfume particles move randomly from where they are concentrated to where they are less concentrated."],
["What happens to the particles when a solid is heated?","They vibrate faster and move slightly further apart","They get bigger","They stop moving","They turn into different particles","The particles gain energy. They stay the same size, but the gaps grow, so the solid expands."],
["Water vapour forms droplets on a cold glass. What is this change called?","Condensation","Evaporation","Melting","Sublimation","Gas cools and turns into liquid on the cold surface."]
]);
T("chem","l1-atoms","Atoms and molecules",1,[
["Which particles are found in the nucleus of an atom?","Protons and neutrons","Electrons only","Protons and electrons","Neutrons and electrons","The nucleus holds protons (+) and neutrons (no charge). Electrons (−) move around it."],
["Why is an atom neutral overall?","It has equal numbers of protons and electrons","It has no protons","It has more neutrons than protons","Its electrons have no charge","The positive and negative charges cancel out."],
["What is a molecule?","Two or more atoms chemically joined together","A single proton","A mixture of elements","A charged particle","For example O₂ is two oxygen atoms joined; H₂O is two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom."],
["How many atoms are in one molecule of CO₂?","3","2","1","4","One carbon atom and two oxygen atoms: 1 + 2 = 3."],
["What does the formula H₂O tell you?","Each molecule has 2 hydrogen atoms and 1 oxygen atom","It has 2 molecules of water","It contains 2 oxygen atoms","It is an element","The small number after H shows two hydrogen atoms; no number means one."],
["Oxygen gas (O₂) is…","an element made of molecules","a compound","a mixture","a single atom","O₂ molecules contain only oxygen atoms, so oxygen is an element."]
]);
T("phy","l1-light","Ray model of light",1,[
["What does the law of reflection state?","Angle of incidence = angle of reflection","Light always bends towards the normal","Angle of reflection is double the angle of incidence","Light is absorbed by mirrors","Both angles are measured from the normal, and they are equal."],
["How far behind a plane mirror is the image of an object 30 cm in front of it?","30 cm","15 cm","60 cm","0 cm","A plane mirror image is the same distance behind the mirror as the object is in front."],
["What happens when light passes from air into glass at an angle?","It slows down and bends towards the normal","It speeds up and bends away from the normal","It does not bend","It reflects completely","Glass is optically denser, so light slows down and bends towards the normal."],
["A white light passes through a prism and splits into colours. This is called…","Dispersion","Reflection","Diffusion","Absorption","Different colours bend by different amounts, spreading out into a spectrum."],
["Why does a red shirt look red in white light?","It reflects red light and absorbs the other colours","It absorbs red light","It makes red light","It reflects all colours","We see the colour that is reflected into our eyes."],
["Which describes the image in a plane mirror?","Upright, same size, laterally inverted, virtual","Inverted and real","Larger and upright","Smaller and real","Mirror images are left-right reversed and cannot be caught on a screen (virtual)."]
]);

/* ===================== YEAR 2 ===================== */
T("bio","l2-digestive","Human digestive system",2,[
["Where does digestion of starch begin?","Mouth","Stomach","Large intestine","Gall bladder","Salivary amylase in the mouth starts breaking starch into sugars."],
["Where are most digested food molecules absorbed into the blood?","Small intestine","Stomach","Large intestine","Oesophagus","The small intestine has villi, which give a large surface area for absorption."],
["What is the main job of the large intestine?","Absorb water","Digest proteins","Produce bile","Make enzymes for starch","It absorbs water from undigested material, forming faeces."],
["What do enzymes do in digestion?","Speed up the breakdown of large food molecules into small ones","Kill all bacteria","Absorb food","Push food along the gut","Enzymes are biological catalysts. They speed up digestion without being used up."],
["What is peristalsis?","Wave-like muscle contractions that push food along the gut","Chewing with the teeth","The absorption of water","Breaking fats with bile","Muscles in the gut wall contract and relax in waves to move food."],
["Proteins are broken down into…","Amino acids","Glucose","Fatty acids only","Starch","Protease enzymes break proteins into amino acids, which the body uses to build cells."]
]);
T("bio","l2-transport","Transport in plants and humans",2,[
["Which vessels carry water from the roots to the leaves?","Xylem","Phloem","Arteries","Veins","Xylem carries water and mineral salts upwards. Phloem carries food (sugars)."],
["What is transpiration?","Loss of water vapour from the leaves","Making food in the leaves","Taking in carbon dioxide","Moving sugar to the roots","Water evaporates from leaf cells and diffuses out through the stomata."],
["Which blood vessels carry blood away from the heart?","Arteries","Veins","Capillaries","Xylem","Arteries carry blood away from the heart at high pressure and have thick walls."],
["What is the function of red blood cells?","Carry oxygen","Fight germs","Clot the blood","Carry digested food only","Red blood cells contain haemoglobin, which carries oxygen around the body."],
["Why do capillaries have walls only one cell thick?","So substances can diffuse in and out quickly","To make them strong","To carry blood at high pressure","To stop blood leaking","Thin walls give a short distance for exchange between blood and cells."],
["What would make a plant transpire faster?","Hot, dry and windy weather","Cold, still air","High humidity","Darkness","Heat, dry air and wind all speed up the evaporation of water from the leaves."]
]);
T("bio","l2-reproduction","Human reproductive system",2,[
["Where are sperm cells made?","Testes","Ovaries","Uterus","Bladder","The testes produce sperm and the male sex hormone testosterone."],
["Where does fertilisation usually happen?","In the oviduct (fallopian tube)","In the uterus","In the ovary","In the vagina","A sperm meets the egg in the oviduct, and the fertilised egg moves to the uterus."],
["What happens during menstruation?","The uterus lining breaks down and leaves the body","An egg is fertilised","The baby is born","Sperm is made","If no fertilisation happens, the thickened uterus lining is shed."],
["What is the function of the placenta?","Exchange food, oxygen and wastes between mother and fetus","Make eggs","Produce sperm","Store urine","The placenta lets substances pass between mother's blood and baby's blood without them mixing."],
["Which of these is a change during puberty?","Body hair grows and the body develops sexually","Height stops changing completely","Heart stops growing","The body stops making hormones","Hormones cause physical changes such as growth spurts, body hair and voice changes."],
["Which method helps protect against sexually transmitted infections?","Condoms","Contraceptive pills","Counting days in the cycle","Exercise","Barrier methods like condoms reduce the spread of STIs. Pills only prevent pregnancy."]
]);
T("bio","l2-ecosystems","Ecosystems and interactions",2,[
["In a food chain, what do the arrows show?","The direction energy flows","What eats the arrow's tip","Which animal is bigger","The order of extinction","Arrows point from the organism eaten to the organism that eats it."],
["What is a producer?","An organism that makes its own food by photosynthesis","An animal that eats plants","An organism that breaks down dead matter","A top predator","Producers, usually green plants, start every food chain."],
["Why does energy decrease along a food chain?","Energy is lost as heat and in movement and wastes at each level","Animals eat too much","Plants make less energy at night","Energy is destroyed","Only about 10% of energy passes to the next level."],
["What do decomposers do?","Break down dead organisms and return nutrients to the soil","Make food from sunlight","Hunt other animals","Pollinate flowers","Bacteria and fungi recycle nutrients so plants can use them again."],
["A bee feeds on nectar and carries pollen between flowers. This relationship is…","Mutualism (both benefit)","Parasitism","Predation","Competition","The bee gets food and the plant gets pollinated, so both benefit."],
["Which human activity increases carbon dioxide in the air?","Burning fossil fuels","Planting trees","Recycling paper","Using solar panels","Combustion of coal, oil and gas releases CO₂, which adds to global warming."]
]);
T("phy","l2-electric","Electrical systems",2,[
["What is electric current?","The rate of flow of charge","The push that drives charge","The opposition to charge flow","The energy used per hour","Current is measured in amperes (A) using an ammeter connected in series."],
["How is a voltmeter connected?","In parallel across the component","In series with the component","Anywhere in the circuit","Only to the battery","A voltmeter measures the potential difference across a component, so it goes in parallel."],
["A resistor has 6 V across it and 2 A through it. What is its resistance?","3 Ω","12 Ω","8 Ω","0.33 Ω","R = V ÷ I = 6 ÷ 2 = 3 Ω."],
["In a parallel circuit, if one bulb breaks, the others…","Stay lit","All go out","Get dimmer","Flash","Each branch is a separate path, so the other bulbs still have a complete circuit."],
["What does a fuse do?","Melts and breaks the circuit if the current is too large","Increases the current","Stores charge","Measures voltage","A fuse protects wiring and appliances from overheating and fire."],
["A 2 kW heater is used for 3 hours. Electricity costs 30 cents per kWh. What is the cost?","$1.80","$0.60","$6.00","$1.50","Energy = 2 kW × 3 h = 6 kWh; cost = 6 × 30 cents = 180 cents = $1.80."]
]);
T("phy","l2-forces","Forces and their effects",2,[
["What is the SI unit of force?","Newton (N)","Kilogram (kg)","Joule (J)","Watt (W)","Force is measured in newtons, using a spring balance (force meter)."],
["A student has a mass of 50 kg. What is her weight on Earth? (g = 10 N/kg)","500 N","50 N","5 N","5000 N","Weight = mass × g = 50 × 10 = 500 N."],
["Why is it easier to walk on a dry floor than an icy one?","There is more friction on the dry floor","There is less gravity on the dry floor","Ice has more friction","Your weight is lower on dry floors","Friction between your shoes and the floor stops you slipping."],
["A force of 10 N acts 0.5 m from a pivot. What is the moment?","5 N m","20 N m","10.5 N m","0.05 N m","Moment = force × perpendicular distance = 10 × 0.5 = 5 N m."],
["Why do knives cut better when they are sharp?","The force acts on a small area, giving high pressure","They are heavier","They have more friction","Sharp edges reduce the force","Pressure = force ÷ area. A smaller area gives a larger pressure."],
["If the forces on an object are balanced, the object…","stays at rest or moves at a constant speed","always speeds up","always slows down","changes direction","Balanced forces mean no change in motion."]
]);
T("phy","l2-energy","Energy, work and power",2,[
["What does the principle of conservation of energy say?","Energy cannot be created or destroyed, only converted","Energy is always lost","Energy can be created by machines","Energy only exists as heat","The total energy stays the same; it changes from one form to another."],
["What energy change happens in a torch?","Chemical → electrical → light (and heat)","Light → chemical","Kinetic → chemical","Heat → electrical","The battery's chemical energy becomes electrical energy, then light and some heat."],
["A box is pushed 4 m with a force of 25 N. How much work is done?","100 J","29 J","6.25 J","21 J","Work = force × distance = 25 × 4 = 100 J."],
["A motor does 600 J of work in 20 s. What is its power?","30 W","12 000 W","580 W","0.03 W","Power = work ÷ time = 600 ÷ 20 = 30 W."],
["Which is a renewable energy source?","Solar energy","Coal","Natural gas","Petrol","Solar energy will not run out, unlike fossil fuels."],
["A ball is held high above the ground. What energy does it have because of its height?","Gravitational potential energy","Kinetic energy","Sound energy","Chemical energy","As it falls, this changes to kinetic energy."]
]);
T("phy","l2-heat","Heat and its transfer",2,[
["How is heat transferred through a metal spoon?","Conduction","Convection","Radiation","Evaporation","Vibrating particles pass energy to neighbouring particles, and free electrons in metals help too."],
["How does heat spread through the water in a pot?","Convection currents","Conduction only","Radiation only","Reflection","Warm water expands, becomes less dense and rises; cooler water sinks to replace it."],
["How does heat from the Sun reach the Earth?","Radiation","Conduction","Convection","Diffusion","Infrared radiation can travel through the vacuum of space; conduction and convection need particles."],
["Which surface is the best absorber of radiant heat?","Dull black","Shiny white","Shiny silver","Smooth white","Dull, dark surfaces absorb (and emit) radiation best."],
["Why are gaps left between sections of railway track?","To allow the metal to expand on hot days","To save metal","To make the train quieter","To let rain drain away","Metals expand when heated. Without gaps the track could buckle."],
["What is the difference between heat and temperature?","Heat is energy transferred; temperature measures how hot something is","They are the same thing","Temperature is measured in joules","Heat is measured in °C","Heat (J) flows from hotter to colder objects; temperature (°C) shows how hot an object is."]
]);
T("chem","l2-changes","Chemical changes",2,[
["Which is a sign that a chemical change has happened?","A gas is given off or a new substance forms","Ice melts","Sugar dissolves","Water boils","Chemical changes make new substances and usually cannot be easily reversed."],
["Which is a physical change?","Melting chocolate","Burning wood","Rusting iron","Cooking an egg","Melting changes state but no new substance forms."],
["What does iron need to rust?","Oxygen and water","Only heat","Only salt","Carbon dioxide only","Rusting is an oxidation reaction that needs both oxygen (air) and water."],
["What is the pH of a neutral solution?","7","0","14","1","pH below 7 is acidic, above 7 is alkaline, and 7 is neutral."],
["An acid reacts with an alkali to form…","a salt and water","only hydrogen","only carbon dioxide","a stronger acid","This is neutralisation, e.g. hydrochloric acid + sodium hydroxide → sodium chloride + water."],
["Heating copper carbonate makes it break down into copper oxide and carbon dioxide. This is…","Thermal decomposition","Combustion","Neutralisation","Dissolving","One substance breaks into simpler substances when heated."]
]);

/* ===================== LESSONS ===================== */
L("prac-l1-endeavour","Science is a way of finding out how the world works, by asking questions and testing ideas fairly and safely.",[
 {t:"The scientific method",p:"Scientists follow a cycle to answer questions.",b:["Ask a testable question","Make a hypothesis (a prediction with a reason)","Plan a fair test: change one variable, keep the others the same","Collect and record results in a table","Analyse results (often with a graph) and write a conclusion","Evaluate: what could be improved?"]},
 {t:"Variables",p:"The independent variable is what you change. The dependent variable is what you measure. Controlled variables are kept the same so the test is fair."},
 {t:"Staying safe in the lab",b:["Wear safety goggles when heating or using chemicals","Tie back long hair; stand when doing practical work","Use the yellow safety flame when the Bunsen is not heating","Report all spills and breakages to the teacher","Never taste or directly smell chemicals"]},
 {t:"Hazard symbols",p:"Labels warn you of the danger.",b:["Flammable: catches fire easily","Corrosive: destroys skin and materials","Toxic: poisonous","Irritant/harmful: causes rashes or harm"]}
],{q:"A student tests whether temperature affects how fast sugar dissolves. Name the variables.",s:["Independent (what is changed): the temperature of the water.","Dependent (what is measured): the time taken for the sugar to dissolve.","Controlled: same mass of sugar, same volume of water, same stirring."],a:"Change temperature, measure time, keep everything else the same"},
"Only change ONE variable at a time, or you can't tell which change caused the result.");

L("chem-l1-physical","Matter can be described and grouped by its physical properties: things you can observe or measure without making a new substance.",[
 {t:"Common physical properties",b:["Density: how much mass is packed into a volume","Melting and boiling point","Conductivity of heat and electricity","Strength and hardness","Flexibility, malleability (can be hammered), ductility (can be drawn into wires)","Transparency, solubility, magnetism"]},
 {t:"Density",p:"Density tells you whether something floats or sinks. Water's density is 1 g/cm³. Objects less dense than water float.",f:"density = mass ÷ volume"},
 {t:"Measuring volume",p:"For a regular shape, volume = length × breadth × height. For an irregular object, use displacement: the rise in water level in a measuring cylinder equals the object's volume. Read the bottom of the meniscus at eye level."},
 {t:"Choosing materials",p:"Engineers pick materials whose properties suit the job: copper for wires (conducts electricity, ductile), plastic for handles (insulator), glass for windows (transparent)."}
],{q:"A stone of mass 54 g raises the water level from 50 cm³ to 70 cm³. Find its density. Will it float?",s:["Volume = 70 − 50 = 20 cm³.","Density = 54 ÷ 20 = 2.7 g/cm³.","2.7 g/cm³ is more than 1 g/cm³, so it sinks."],a:"2.7 g/cm³; it sinks"},
"Keep units consistent: g with cm³ gives g/cm³; kg with m³ gives kg/m³.");

L("chem-l1-elements","All matter is made of elements, either on their own, joined together as compounds, or mixed together.",[
 {t:"Elements",p:"An element is a pure substance made of only one type of atom. It cannot be broken down into anything simpler by chemical means. There are 118 known elements, arranged in the Periodic Table by proton number.",b:["Metals (left side): shiny, conduct heat and electricity, malleable","Non-metals (right side): dull, poor conductors, brittle when solid"]},
 {t:"Compounds",p:"A compound is two or more elements chemically combined in a fixed ratio, e.g. water (H₂O) and carbon dioxide (CO₂). A compound has different properties from the elements that made it, and needs a chemical reaction to separate."},
 {t:"Mixtures",p:"A mixture contains two or more substances that are not chemically joined, e.g. air, seawater, sand and iron filings. The parts keep their own properties, the amounts can vary, and they can be separated by physical methods."},
 {t:"Spot the difference",b:["Element: one type of atom (O₂, Fe)","Compound: chemically joined, fixed ratio (H₂O, NaCl)","Mixture: not joined, any ratio (air, salt water)"]}
],{q:"Iron filings and sulfur powder are mixed, then heated to make iron sulfide. Compare the mixture and the product.",s:["The mixture: iron can be pulled out with a magnet; the ratio can be anything.","After heating, a new compound, iron sulfide, forms. It is not magnetic.","The compound has a fixed ratio and can't be separated with a magnet."],a:"Mixture separates with a magnet; the compound doesn't"},
"Oxygen gas (O₂) is an element even though it's made of molecules: all its atoms are oxygen.");

L("chem-l1-separation","Mixtures can be separated by physical methods. Pick the method based on the properties of the substances.",[
 {t:"Insoluble solid from a liquid",p:"Filtration: the insoluble solid stays on the filter paper as the residue, and the liquid passing through is the filtrate. E.g. sand from water."},
 {t:"Dissolved solid from a solution",p:"Evaporation heats the solution until all the water is gone. Crystallisation heats until the solution is saturated, then leaves it to cool so crystals form: better for salts that decompose when heated strongly."},
 {t:"Liquids",b:["Simple distillation: get the solvent (e.g. pure water) from a solution","Fractional distillation: separate liquids with different boiling points (e.g. ethanol and water)","Separating funnel: separate liquids that don't mix (e.g. oil and water)"]},
 {t:"Chromatography",p:"Separates dyes or colours in a mixture. Spots move up the paper with the solvent; more soluble dyes travel further. A pure substance gives only one spot."},
 {t:"Other methods",b:["Magnetic attraction: iron from other solids","Sieving: different sized solids","Decanting: pour off liquid from settled solid"]}
],{q:"How would you get pure, dry salt from a mixture of salt and sand?",s:["Add water and stir: salt dissolves, sand doesn't.","Filter: sand stays as the residue, salt solution is the filtrate.","Evaporate or crystallise the filtrate to get salt crystals, then dry them."],a:"Dissolve, filter, then evaporate/crystallise"},
"Filtration only removes INSOLUBLE solids. It can't take dissolved salt out of water.");

L("bio-l1-diversity","There are millions of kinds of living things. Classification sorts them into groups by shared characteristics.",[
 {t:"Why classify?",p:"Grouping organisms makes them easier to identify, study and talk about. Scientists group them by observable characteristics, such as body structure, how they reproduce and how they feed."},
 {t:"Main groups",b:["Animals: vertebrates (with backbones) and invertebrates (without)","Plants: flowering and non-flowering (ferns, mosses)","Fungi: feed on dead or living matter, no chlorophyll","Bacteria: tiny single-celled organisms"]},
 {t:"Vertebrates",b:["Fish: scales, gills, fins","Amphibians: moist skin, live in water and on land","Reptiles: dry scaly skin, lay eggs on land","Birds: feathers, beaks, lay hard-shelled eggs","Mammals: fur or hair, feed young with milk"]},
 {t:"Dichotomous keys",p:"A key asks a series of questions, each with two answers. You follow the choices until you reach the organism's name. 'Dichotomous' means dividing into two."}
],{q:"A key says: 1a Has 6 legs → go to 2; 1b Has 8 legs → Spider. 2a Has wings → Housefly; 2b No wings → Ant. Identify an animal with 6 legs and no wings.",s:["Step 1: it has 6 legs, so go to 2.","Step 2: it has no wings.","So it is the ant."],a:"Ant"},
"Bats fly but are mammals, and whales swim but are mammals too: classify by key features like fur and milk, not lifestyle.");

L("bio-l1-cells","The cell is the basic unit of life. All living things are made of one or more cells.",[
 {t:"Parts of a cell",b:["Nucleus: controls the cell and contains genetic material (DNA)","Cytoplasm: jelly where chemical reactions happen","Cell membrane: controls what enters and leaves","Mitochondria: release energy by respiration"]},
 {t:"Plant cells also have",b:["Cell wall (cellulose): gives a fixed shape and support","Chloroplasts: contain chlorophyll for photosynthesis","Large central vacuole: stores cell sap and keeps the cell firm"]},
 {t:"Specialised cells",p:"Cells have shapes suited to their jobs.",b:["Red blood cell: no nucleus, biconcave shape, carries oxygen","Root hair cell: long extension for absorbing water","Nerve cell: long fibres to carry messages","Sperm cell: tail for swimming"]},
 {t:"Levels of organisation",p:"Cell → tissue (similar cells) → organ (several tissues) → organ system → organism. E.g. muscle cells → muscle tissue → heart → circulatory system → human."}
],{q:"A cell has a cell wall and a nucleus but no chloroplasts. Could it be from a plant?",s:["Animal cells have no cell wall, so it isn't an animal cell.","Plant cells have walls; root cells live underground and have no chloroplasts.","So it could be a plant root cell."],a:"Yes, e.g. a root cell"},
"Not every plant cell has chloroplasts: root cells don't, because they get no light.");

L("chem-l1-particles","All matter is made of tiny particles that are always moving. This particulate model explains solids, liquids and gases.",[
 {t:"Three states",b:["Solid: particles closely packed in a regular pattern, vibrate in fixed positions; fixed shape and volume","Liquid: close together but disordered, slide past each other; fixed volume, takes the shape of its container","Gas: far apart, moving fast and randomly; no fixed shape or volume, easily compressed"]},
 {t:"Changes of state",b:["Melting: solid → liquid","Boiling/evaporation: liquid → gas","Condensation: gas → liquid","Freezing: liquid → solid","Sublimation: solid → gas directly (e.g. dry ice)"]},
 {t:"Heating and expansion",p:"Heating gives particles more energy. They move faster and spread further apart, so the substance expands. The particles themselves do NOT get bigger."},
 {t:"Diffusion",p:"Particles spread from a region of higher concentration to lower concentration because they move randomly. Diffusion is faster in gases than liquids, and faster at higher temperatures."}
],{q:"Why does a balloon left in the hot sun get bigger?",s:["The gas particles inside gain energy from the heat.","They move faster and hit the balloon wall harder and more often.","The gas pushes outwards more, so the balloon expands."],a:"Faster particles push the walls out more"},
"Particles don't expand when heated. The spaces between them get bigger.");

L("chem-l1-atoms","Atoms are the building blocks of all matter. They join together to form molecules.",[
 {t:"Inside an atom",b:["Proton: positive charge, in the nucleus","Neutron: no charge, in the nucleus","Electron: negative charge, moves around the nucleus in shells"]},
 {t:"Neutral atoms",p:"An atom has the same number of protons and electrons, so the charges cancel. The number of protons (the proton number) decides which element it is: every carbon atom has 6 protons."},
 {t:"Molecules",p:"A molecule is two or more atoms chemically joined. They can be the same element (O₂, N₂) or different elements (H₂O, CO₂)."},
 {t:"Reading chemical formulae",p:"Symbols show the elements; small numbers show how many atoms. CO₂ = 1 carbon + 2 oxygen. H₂SO₄ = 2 hydrogen + 1 sulfur + 4 oxygen = 7 atoms."}
],{q:"How many atoms of each element are in one molecule of glucose, C₆H₁₂O₆?",s:["C₆: 6 carbon atoms.","H₁₂: 12 hydrogen atoms.","O₆: 6 oxygen atoms; 24 atoms in total."],a:"6 C, 12 H, 6 O (24 atoms)"},
"In 2H₂O the big 2 means two molecules, so there are 4 hydrogen atoms and 2 oxygen atoms.");

L("phy-l1-light","We model light as rays: straight lines with arrows showing the direction light travels.",[
 {t:"Reflection",p:"When light hits a mirror it bounces off. Angles are measured from the normal (a line at 90° to the surface).",f:"angle of incidence = angle of reflection"},
 {t:"Plane mirror images",b:["Same size as the object","Upright","Laterally inverted (left-right reversed)","Virtual: can't be caught on a screen","As far behind the mirror as the object is in front"]},
 {t:"Refraction",p:"Light changes speed when it moves from one material to another, so it bends. Into a denser material (air → glass/water) it slows and bends towards the normal. Out into a less dense material it speeds up and bends away. This is why a straw looks bent in water."},
 {t:"Colour",p:"White light is a mix of colours. A prism splits it into a spectrum (red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, violet): dispersion. Objects look coloured because they reflect that colour and absorb the others."}
],{q:"A light ray hits a mirror at 35° to the mirror's surface. What is the angle of reflection?",s:["Angles are measured from the normal, not the surface.","Angle of incidence = 90° − 35° = 55°.","Angle of reflection = angle of incidence = 55°."],a:"55°"},
"Always measure angles from the NORMAL, not from the mirror or surface.");

L("bio-l2-digestive","Digestion breaks big, insoluble food molecules into small, soluble ones that can be absorbed into the blood.",[
 {t:"Two kinds of digestion",p:"Physical digestion breaks food into smaller pieces (teeth chewing, stomach churning). Chemical digestion uses enzymes to break large molecules into small molecules."},
 {t:"The journey of food",b:["Mouth: teeth chew; saliva with amylase starts digesting starch","Oesophagus: peristalsis pushes food to the stomach","Stomach: acid kills germs; protease starts protein digestion","Small intestine: digestion finishes; villi absorb nutrients into the blood","Large intestine: water is absorbed","Rectum and anus: faeces are stored and removed"]},
 {t:"Enzymes",b:["Amylase: starch → sugars","Protease: proteins → amino acids","Lipase: fats → fatty acids and glycerol"]},
 {t:"Helpers",p:"The liver makes bile, which is stored in the gall bladder and breaks fat into small droplets. The pancreas makes enzymes that are released into the small intestine."}
],{q:"Why is the small intestine long and lined with villi?",s:["Absorption needs a large surface area.","The great length and millions of finger-like villi give a huge surface.","Villi have thin walls and many capillaries, so nutrients enter the blood quickly."],a:"Huge surface area for fast absorption"},
"Bile is not an enzyme. It breaks fat into droplets so lipase can work faster.");

L("bio-l2-transport","Living things need to move water, food, oxygen and wastes around. Plants and humans have transport systems to do this.",[
 {t:"Transport in plants",b:["Xylem: carries water and mineral salts from the roots up to the leaves","Phloem: carries food (sugars) made in the leaves to other parts of the plant","Root hairs absorb water from the soil"]},
 {t:"Transpiration",p:"Water evaporates from leaf cells and escapes as vapour through the stomata. This pulls more water up the xylem. It is faster when it is hot, dry, windy and bright."},
 {t:"Human circulatory system",p:"The heart pumps blood around the body through blood vessels.",b:["Arteries: carry blood away from the heart; thick, muscular walls","Veins: carry blood back to the heart; have valves","Capillaries: tiny vessels with walls one cell thick, where exchange happens"]},
 {t:"Blood",b:["Red blood cells: carry oxygen (haemoglobin)","White blood cells: fight germs","Platelets: help blood clot","Plasma: liquid that carries dissolved food, CO₂ and hormones"]}
],{q:"A plant's leaves are covered in petroleum jelly on the underside. Predict what happens to its water loss and explain.",s:["Most stomata are on the underside of leaves.","The jelly blocks the stomata, so water vapour can't escape.","Transpiration falls, and less water is pulled up the xylem."],a:"Water loss drops because the stomata are blocked"},
"Arteries carry blood AWAY from the heart (A for away), not always oxygen-rich blood.");

L("bio-l2-reproduction","Humans reproduce sexually: a sperm from the father fertilises an egg from the mother.",[
 {t:"Male reproductive system",b:["Testes: make sperm and testosterone","Sperm duct: carries sperm","Glands: add fluid to make semen","Penis and urethra: carry semen out"]},
 {t:"Female reproductive system",b:["Ovaries: release an egg about once a month, make oestrogen","Oviduct: where fertilisation happens","Uterus: where the fetus develops","Cervix and vagina"]},
 {t:"Menstrual cycle (about 28 days)",p:"The uterus lining thickens; an egg is released around day 14 (ovulation). If the egg is not fertilised, the lining breaks down and leaves the body (menstruation)."},
 {t:"Fertilisation to birth",p:"A sperm nucleus fuses with an egg nucleus in the oviduct to form a zygote. It divides into an embryo, which implants in the uterus lining. The placenta and umbilical cord supply food and oxygen and remove wastes until birth."},
 {t:"Puberty and health",p:"During puberty, hormones bring physical and emotional changes. Some infections spread through sexual contact (STIs, e.g. HIV). Abstinence and condoms reduce the risk."}
],{q:"Why can't a woman become pregnant during menstruation in a typical cycle?",s:["Menstruation is around days 1–5 of the cycle.","The egg is usually released around day 14.","With no egg in the oviduct then, fertilisation is unlikely (but cycles can vary)."],a:"No egg is usually available then"},
"Fertilisation happens in the OVIDUCT; the embryo develops in the uterus.");

L("bio-l2-ecosystems","An ecosystem is all the living things in an area plus their non-living environment, all interacting.",[
 {t:"Key words",b:["Habitat: where an organism lives","Population: all organisms of one species in an area","Community: all populations in an area","Ecosystem: the community plus its environment (light, water, soil, temperature)"]},
 {t:"Food chains and webs",p:"Energy starts with the Sun. Producers (plants) make food by photosynthesis. Primary consumers eat producers, secondary consumers eat them. A food web links many food chains. Arrows show the direction of energy flow."},
 {t:"Energy and nutrients",p:"Only about 10% of energy passes to the next level; the rest is lost as heat, movement and waste. Decomposers (bacteria, fungi) break down dead matter so nutrients return to the soil."},
 {t:"Relationships",b:["Predation: one eats another","Competition: for food, space, light or mates","Mutualism: both benefit (bees and flowers)","Parasitism: one benefits, the host is harmed (tapeworm)"]},
 {t:"Human impact",p:"Deforestation, pollution and burning fossil fuels harm ecosystems and add CO₂, causing climate change. Conservation, recycling and renewable energy help."}
],{q:"In the chain grass → grasshopper → frog → snake, what happens if the frogs die out?",s:["Grasshoppers lose their predator, so their numbers rise.","More grasshoppers eat more grass, so the grass decreases.","Snakes lose a food source, so their numbers fall."],a:"Grasshoppers up, grass down, snakes down"},
"Arrows point to the EATER, showing where energy goes, not 'what eats what'.");

L("phy-l2-electric","Electric circuits carry energy from a source, like a battery, to components such as bulbs and motors.",[
 {t:"Current, voltage and resistance",b:["Current (I): flow of charge, in amperes (A), measured with an ammeter in series","Potential difference (V): the push, in volts (V), measured with a voltmeter in parallel","Resistance (R): opposition to current, in ohms (Ω)"],f:"R = V ÷ I"},
 {t:"Series circuits",p:"One path. The current is the same everywhere. If one bulb breaks, everything goes off. Adding bulbs makes each dimmer."},
 {t:"Parallel circuits",p:"Several paths (branches). The current splits between branches. Each branch gets the full voltage, and if one bulb breaks the others stay on. Houses are wired in parallel."},
 {t:"Safety and cost",p:"Fuses and circuit breakers cut the current if it gets too large. Earth wires protect you from shocks. Electrical energy is paid for in kilowatt-hours (kWh).",f:"energy (kWh) = power (kW) × time (h)"}
],{q:"A 1.5 kW kettle is used for 20 minutes a day for 30 days. At 30 cents per kWh, find the cost.",s:["Time = 20 min × 30 = 600 min = 10 h.","Energy = 1.5 kW × 10 h = 15 kWh.","Cost = 15 × 30 cents = 450 cents = $4.50."],a:"$4.50"},
"Convert minutes to hours and watts to kilowatts before using kWh.");

L("phy-l2-forces","A force is a push or a pull. Forces can change an object's speed, direction or shape.",[
 {t:"Types of forces",b:["Contact forces: friction, air resistance, tension, normal reaction","Non-contact forces: gravity (weight), magnetic, electrostatic"]},
 {t:"Mass and weight",p:"Mass is the amount of matter (kg) and is the same everywhere. Weight is the pull of gravity on that mass (N) and changes on other planets.",f:"weight = mass × g (g = 10 N/kg on Earth)"},
 {t:"Balanced and unbalanced forces",p:"Balanced forces (resultant force zero): the object stays still or keeps moving at a steady speed. Unbalanced forces change its motion: it speeds up, slows down or turns."},
 {t:"Turning effect and pressure",b:["Moment = force × perpendicular distance from pivot (N m)","Pressure = force ÷ area (N/m² or Pa)","Sharp knives: small area, high pressure","Wide tyres and snowshoes: large area, low pressure"]}
],{q:"A 60 kg student stands on one foot with area 0.02 m². Find the pressure on the floor.",s:["Weight = 60 × 10 = 600 N.","Pressure = force ÷ area = 600 ÷ 0.02.","Pressure = 30 000 Pa."],a:"30 000 Pa"},
"Mass is in kg and weight is in N. Your mass on the Moon is the same, but your weight is less.");

L("phy-l2-energy","Energy makes things happen. It is never used up, only changed from one form into another.",[
 {t:"Forms of energy",b:["Kinetic: moving objects","Gravitational potential: objects raised up","Chemical: in food, fuels and batteries","Elastic potential: stretched or squashed objects","Electrical, light, sound, thermal (heat), nuclear"]},
 {t:"Conservation of energy",p:"Energy cannot be created or destroyed. In every change some energy is 'wasted', usually as heat or sound, but the total stays the same. E.g. a falling ball: GPE → KE."},
 {t:"Work and power",p:"Work is done when a force moves an object. Power is how fast work is done.",f:"work (J) = force (N) × distance (m);  power (W) = work ÷ time (s)"},
 {t:"Energy resources",b:["Non-renewable: coal, oil, natural gas (fossil fuels), nuclear","Renewable: solar, wind, hydroelectric, tidal, geothermal, biomass","Fossil fuels release CO₂, adding to climate change"]}
],{q:"A girl of weight 500 N climbs stairs 3 m high in 6 s. Find the work done and her power.",s:["Work = force × distance = 500 × 3 = 1500 J.","Power = work ÷ time = 1500 ÷ 6.","Power = 250 W."],a:"1500 J; 250 W"},
"'Energy is lost' really means it spreads out as heat. It isn't destroyed.");

L("phy-l2-heat","Heat is energy that flows from a hotter place to a colder place. It moves in three ways.",[
 {t:"Heat vs temperature",p:"Heat is energy transferred, measured in joules (J). Temperature tells you how hot something is, measured in °C with a thermometer. A bath of warm water has more heat energy than a cup of boiling water, even though its temperature is lower."},
 {t:"Conduction",p:"Particles vibrate and pass energy to their neighbours. Metals are good conductors (they also have free electrons). Plastic, wood, air and wool are poor conductors, called insulators."},
 {t:"Convection",p:"In liquids and gases, heated fluid expands, becomes less dense and rises; cooler fluid sinks. This makes a convection current. It's why air conditioners are high on the wall and heaters are low."},
 {t:"Radiation",p:"Infrared waves carry heat, even through a vacuum. Dull black surfaces absorb and emit radiation best; shiny white or silver surfaces reflect it."},
 {t:"Effects of heat",p:"Most things expand when heated and contract when cooled. Gaps in railway tracks and bridges allow for expansion. Heat can also cause changes of state."}
],{q:"Why does a vacuum flask keep drinks hot?",s:["The vacuum between the walls stops conduction and convection (no particles).","The silvered walls reflect radiation back.","The stopper reduces heat loss by convection and evaporation."],a:"It cuts all three ways heat escapes"},
"'Heat rises' isn't quite right: hot liquids and gases rise by convection, but heat itself can travel in any direction.");

L("chem-l2-changes","In a chemical change, new substances are formed. In a physical change, no new substance forms.",[
 {t:"Physical vs chemical",b:["Physical: change of state, dissolving, cutting; usually easy to reverse","Chemical: new substance formed; usually hard to reverse","Signs of a chemical change: gas given off, colour change, heat or light, precipitate forms"]},
 {t:"Types of chemical change",b:["Combustion: burning in oxygen, e.g. methane + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water","Thermal decomposition: breaking down by heat, e.g. copper carbonate → copper oxide + carbon dioxide","Oxidation: reacting with oxygen, e.g. rusting needs oxygen and water","Neutralisation: acid + alkali → salt + water"]},
 {t:"Acids, alkalis and pH",p:"The pH scale runs from 0 to 14. Below 7 is acidic, 7 is neutral, above 7 is alkaline. Universal indicator shows pH by colour: red for strong acid, green for neutral, purple for strong alkali. Litmus turns red in acid and blue in alkali."},
 {t:"Preventing rust",p:"Stop oxygen or water reaching the iron: paint, oil, grease, plastic coating or galvanising (zinc coating)."}
],{q:"Toothpaste is slightly alkaline. Why does it help protect teeth?",s:["Bacteria in the mouth make acids from sugary food.","Acids attack tooth enamel.","Alkaline toothpaste neutralises the acid."],a:"It neutralises acids in the mouth"},
"Dissolving is a PHYSICAL change: evaporate the water and you get the sugar or salt back.");
